1
00:00:00,031 --> 00:00:28,097
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

2
00:00:30,236 --> 00:00:36,704
Okay, so we're tackling this word upadana.

3
00:00:37,405 --> 00:00:42,832
So it's translated as clinging, sometimes, oopsie, glasses, sometimes grasping.

4
00:00:44,774 --> 00:00:46,877
Trying to get the thing off my glasses there.

5
00:00:49,039 --> 00:00:49,840
The shine off.

6
00:00:50,341 --> 00:00:51,942
Oh dear me.

7
00:00:53,064 --> 00:00:53,765
How's that?

8
00:00:53,784 --> 00:00:54,625
Okay.

9
00:00:54,646 --> 00:00:55,366
Le lunette.

10
00:00:55,938 --> 00:00:57,399
Half glassy.

11
00:00:58,521 --> 00:01:02,045
So it's translated as clinging, sometimes grasping.

12
00:01:02,466 --> 00:01:06,572
It's important because it's the eighth link on dependence origination, right?

13
00:01:06,593 --> 00:01:18,468
Now, it's literal meaning, according to the Pali dictionary, that material substratum by means of which an active process is kept alive and going.

14
00:01:18,954 --> 00:01:20,799
Fuel, supply, provision.

15
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:28,233
So in other words, its role in the psyche is the same as something like petrol or electricity in the car.

16
00:01:28,495 --> 00:01:30,037
Yeah, it sustains the process.

17
00:01:31,239 --> 00:01:32,884
So there are four of these clingings.

18
00:01:35,829 --> 00:01:37,393
The Dharma is very good at lists.

19
00:01:37,653 --> 00:01:38,736
So we've got four of these.

20
00:01:39,965 --> 00:01:53,959
The first one won't surprise you, clinging to sensual pleasure, clinging to rites and rituals as if they were liberating, clinging to views and opinions, and finally, of course, clinging to the view of self.

21
00:01:55,341 --> 00:02:02,530
So if we look at the first one, most of you will know this, you know, it's the desire for sensual pleasure.

22
00:02:02,829 --> 00:02:09,296
But remember that there are six senses in the Buddhist teaching, and the sixth, of course, is the mind itself.

23
00:02:09,849 --> 00:02:17,578
So it means all our emotions, all our thoughts, etc., which is dependent usually on some physical base.

24
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:26,647
And the problem is that, of course, when we're driven by this sort of desire, it's never fulfilling, right?

25
00:02:26,668 --> 00:02:31,152
As soon as we are fulfilled, we rest for a little while and then we want some more.

26
00:02:32,394 --> 00:02:38,540
And it leads us to manipulating the world we're living in, right, to make it fit into means.

27
00:02:39,044 --> 00:02:49,780
So we're constantly having this little strife or struggle, sometimes quite minor, you know, little irritations and things like that, and sometimes pretty major.

28
00:02:51,042 --> 00:03:06,485
But anything that arises out of that desire to seek happiness in pleasures, in the six sensual pleasures, is obviously going to lead to some level of unsatisfactoriness.

29
00:03:08,812 --> 00:03:09,992
So how do we tackle that?

30
00:03:10,013 --> 00:03:17,379
Well, we know it's through vipassana, to see it clearly, to see that process clearly is the first understanding.

31
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:21,043
And then, of course, there's the practice of not indulging it.

32
00:03:22,503 --> 00:03:28,348
So if we can make a distinction here, when we eat toast, it makes us happy.

33
00:03:30,010 --> 00:03:34,655
But if we eat toast in order to make us happy, there's going to be a problem, okay?

34
00:03:36,697 --> 00:03:37,957
That's the one we don't want.

35
00:03:39,794 --> 00:03:42,796
And the other thing that the Buddha uses is restraint.

36
00:03:42,897 --> 00:03:44,158
He's big on restraint.

37
00:03:45,741 --> 00:03:55,192
So in other words, whenever we feel that compulsive nature over something, doesn't matter what it is, toast, cup of tea, to just hold back and let go of it.

38
00:03:55,431 --> 00:03:58,895
Wait for that over energy to give way.

39
00:03:59,616 --> 00:04:01,479
And if possible, do without one.

40
00:04:04,042 --> 00:04:06,865
You know, it's like when you walk on the street and a

41
00:04:07,030 --> 00:04:13,481
And you come across a cafe and the coffee sort of hooks your nose and reels you in.

42
00:04:14,864 --> 00:04:22,517
It's sort of resisting that and just walks steadfastly past that cafe, at least until the next one.

43
00:04:25,202 --> 00:04:30,372
But it's also really restraining everything which is becoming obsessional.

44
00:04:31,026 --> 00:04:32,428
And don't forget fasting.

45
00:04:32,829 --> 00:04:36,716
So fasting isn't only, you know, not eating if it's become obsessional.

46
00:04:37,117 --> 00:04:42,384
It's all the social media, the news, the videos, the shopping, et cetera, et cetera.

47
00:04:42,906 --> 00:04:49,976
Anything that comes compulsive, we stop for a while just to let go of it, right?

48
00:04:50,999 --> 00:04:56,848
When we come on retreat, the first three days are always difficult or one of the

49
00:04:57,081 --> 00:05:01,428
things that makes the first three days really difficult is the change of habits.

50
00:05:02,670 --> 00:05:13,370
Drinking, getting up at a different hour, not eating at the same time, all these things are irritating.

51
00:05:13,569 --> 00:05:15,934
We don't like to change our habits.

52
00:05:19,038 --> 00:05:23,105
So that's the first clinging, clinging to sensual pleasure.

53
00:05:23,677 --> 00:05:29,704
The next one is clinging to rites and rituals as a religious practice.

54
00:05:30,245 --> 00:05:33,548
Now, some of it is very supportive, for instance, bowing.

55
00:05:34,069 --> 00:05:44,221
So bowing can be an expression of surrender, of gratitude, of a sense of offering, like you're offering your day for the benefit of others.

56
00:05:44,882 --> 00:05:52,992
And it can be a sense of awe, you know, when you bring the profundity of the Buddha's teachings to mind, you know, and say, how did he do it, you know?

57
00:05:54,202 --> 00:06:01,194
Lighting a candle, see, that can remind us of the three paths, inverted commas, within the Eightfold Path.

58
00:06:01,214 --> 00:06:10,411
So the light is the path of insight, the warmth, the path of love, and the action of the flame, the path of wholesome works, good works.

59
00:06:12,495 --> 00:06:18,725
And it's important, you know, to recite the refuges and precepts every morning.

60
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:23,605
because that just reminds you what your life is about, ultimately.

61
00:06:23,764 --> 00:06:25,427
It gives it that deeper meaning.

62
00:06:27,749 --> 00:06:34,235
Chanting, beading, it's all good, you know, to keep the mind steady, to stop it wandering all over the place.

63
00:06:35,677 --> 00:06:47,649
And, of course, taking refuges and precepts as a public declaration is, you know, where you're committing yourself to the path, has a real strengthening effect.

64
00:06:48,052 --> 00:06:51,117
It doesn't mean to say that you might not become a Muslim later on.

65
00:06:51,557 --> 00:06:56,925
It's just that at this present moment, you're devoted to this particular teaching.

66
00:07:00,690 --> 00:07:13,029
So all these practices, I was going to say, actually, it's like sealing a particular relationship with the public right of marriage.

67
00:07:13,245 --> 00:07:18,732
There's the christening, or the naming ceremony, and there's burials.

68
00:07:18,752 --> 00:07:24,577
These are all rituals, they're very meaningful, but in themselves, unfortunately, they don't lead to liberation.

69
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:26,379
Never mind.

70
00:07:28,341 --> 00:07:32,326
Number three, well, this is a big one, clinging to views and opinions.

71
00:07:32,466 --> 00:07:40,214
I mean, that's, you know, our whole politics has now become polarizing because

72
00:07:40,567 --> 00:07:43,971
of people clinging to their views and opinions.

73
00:07:44,692 --> 00:07:51,142
And what happens is that you forget about the issue and you start to attack the person.

74
00:07:51,903 --> 00:07:58,213
These arguments are called ad hominem, argumentum ad hominem.

75
00:07:58,233 --> 00:08:07,687
There's a bit of Latin there, which means that the argument, the speaker attacks the character, the motive, some other attribute of the person.

76
00:08:08,173 --> 00:08:34,114
make who's making the argument forget the issue right as long as they can as long as they can humiliate the other they've won the argument and uh interestingly enough the buddha actually in very early strata of the discourses tells his monks and nuns not to get involved in these open debates so you just come away either with a big ego or you just come away humiliated

77
00:08:36,472 --> 00:08:50,649
Yeah, just on just my own reflection, right up until I think Thatcher came in, politics tended to still be gentlemanly, you know, still patriarchal, so gentlemanly.

78
00:08:51,711 --> 00:09:03,504
But then the names came out, you know, they started calling conservatives who didn't follow her way, the wets, if you remember, people who were unemployed became scroungers.

79
00:09:03,985 --> 00:09:06,327
And so the whole thing began to get

80
00:09:06,476 --> 00:09:08,438
quite ugly, really.

81
00:09:09,779 --> 00:09:16,827
There was Cameron making fun of Farage, making fun of his name, his poncy foreign-sounding pronunciation.

82
00:09:17,467 --> 00:09:18,929
I've got all these little things.

83
00:09:19,029 --> 00:09:22,332
And recently Polanski was made fun of because of his teeth.

84
00:09:24,535 --> 00:09:26,197
Of course, Trump is a master of it.

85
00:09:26,336 --> 00:09:30,081
I mean, once he said Sleepy Joe, really that was the end of it, I think.

86
00:09:31,042 --> 00:09:34,745
So it's sort of drawing attention

87
00:09:35,197 --> 00:09:55,197
politics down to this real real ugly level of just trying to destroy the person and of course it's all over I mean I've just mentioned things like that in the UK but everywhere you go this is what's happening um

88
00:09:55,802 --> 00:10:11,647
So the antidote, of course, is a little bit of humility, really, is to realize that whatever view I have, I can't grasp the whole situation, the whole matrix that the problem is in and that I need other people's perspective.

89
00:10:12,148 --> 00:10:18,957
And the wider my perspective is, the more I can say, well, I'm getting close to understanding the situation.

90
00:10:20,490 --> 00:10:26,080
And sometimes people think that this teaching tells you not to have a view.

91
00:10:26,679 --> 00:10:28,243
Well, remember, the Buddha had a view.

92
00:10:28,403 --> 00:10:29,303
He had right view.

93
00:10:29,985 --> 00:10:31,807
So we can have views.

94
00:10:32,308 --> 00:10:36,154
It's just the way we relate to them in terms of identity.

95
00:10:36,414 --> 00:10:37,677
You know, I am.

96
00:10:38,038 --> 00:10:38,698
I am right.

97
00:10:38,798 --> 00:10:39,759
Everybody else is wrong.

98
00:10:40,421 --> 00:10:41,142
I'm a lefty.

99
00:10:41,202 --> 00:10:44,167
I'm a right winger and so on and so forth.

100
00:10:44,187 --> 00:10:45,469
And there's no need for it.

101
00:10:45,684 --> 00:11:06,883
uh if we come down to just looking at the issues and asking well what can we do here it's as simple as that is what can be done so this is a big problem this uh this polarization and uh it's all it's this clinging you know to my view

102
00:11:08,163 --> 00:11:15,254
The final one, of course, is clinging to the view of self, and this is the root clinging.

103
00:11:15,696 --> 00:11:20,465
From this clinging, all clinging clings, right?

104
00:11:21,025 --> 00:11:29,259
And the problem with the self is it only sees the sensual world, and it only sees the sensual world as a place where it can make itself happy.

105
00:11:29,628 --> 00:11:31,149
So you can't blame it.

106
00:11:31,350 --> 00:11:37,158
You can't blame yourself for wanting to be happy in the sensual world.

107
00:11:37,638 --> 00:11:44,167
And what it does, it obviously collects a lot of stuff, feels safer for it, more money in the bank, etc.

108
00:11:44,726 --> 00:11:48,152
But then that acquisitiveness has to be guarded.

109
00:11:48,631 --> 00:11:53,918
And therefore, you're always on the lookout for danger, governments, people like that, to take all your money away.

110
00:11:55,240 --> 00:11:59,466
And then, of course, if they're bigger than you, like governments, you tend to run, tend to hide.

111
00:12:00,390 --> 00:12:12,408
So you've got this basic triangle, this three-pronged movement into daily life of assertiveness, aversion, and fear.

112
00:12:13,490 --> 00:12:15,953
And from that, all the problems arise.

113
00:12:15,974 --> 00:12:17,615
So how do we undermine that?

114
00:12:17,697 --> 00:12:20,039
Well, obviously, again, it's vipassana.

115
00:12:20,441 --> 00:12:27,149
And I want to point you to this place that we get to sometimes when everything is calm.

116
00:12:27,610 --> 00:12:29,553
The mind is still, everything's calm.

117
00:12:29,871 --> 00:12:40,183
And you become very much aware of being the, you know, I've got all these words, observer, the feeler, the experiencer, the knower, the witness.

118
00:12:40,244 --> 00:12:41,725
It doesn't matter what you call it.

119
00:12:43,768 --> 00:12:51,998
But it's beginning to understand that whatever is perceiving cannot be what is perceived.

120
00:12:52,484 --> 00:12:57,931
So when I look at the shed that's in my garden here, I can't be the shed.

121
00:12:59,192 --> 00:13:04,700
So when I go inwards and I perceive a pain, I can't be it.

122
00:13:04,860 --> 00:13:06,982
It's an object of my perceiving.

123
00:13:07,884 --> 00:13:14,894
And it's making that more and more clear to ourselves that begins to undermine that sense of self.

124
00:13:17,717 --> 00:13:20,922
And we can also undermine it by just practicing virtues.

125
00:13:21,172 --> 00:13:31,264
Anything that undermines that sense of ego, that sense of selfishness, generosity, love, compassion, sympathetic joy and so on.

126
00:13:31,283 --> 00:13:41,856
All these, when we're practicing virtues, we must be undermining the sense of self to simple as that.

127
00:13:41,876 --> 00:13:50,587
And finally, just this core belief, this core, sorry, this core insight that the Buddha gives us about the self.

128
00:13:51,580 --> 00:13:57,308
He saw clearly that the identity comes after the wanting.

129
00:13:58,049 --> 00:13:59,893
And it's language what fools us.

130
00:14:00,614 --> 00:14:03,518
I say, you know, I want a coffee.

131
00:14:04,259 --> 00:14:08,306
So the I comes first and then the want and then the coffee.

132
00:14:09,067 --> 00:14:12,913
But psychologically, it's coffee, want, I.

133
00:14:13,365 --> 00:14:16,951
And this allows us to just distance from the want.

134
00:14:17,231 --> 00:14:21,879
We can actually see the wanting as an object, some feeling within us.

135
00:14:23,020 --> 00:14:25,244
And it gives us just one moment of choice.

136
00:14:25,303 --> 00:14:27,707
It's the only moment we have of choice.

137
00:14:28,148 --> 00:14:30,471
Now this isn't some airy-fairy free will.

138
00:14:30,511 --> 00:14:32,595
This choice is conditioned.

139
00:14:32,674 --> 00:14:35,499
It's conditioned by wisdom or lack of it.

140
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:39,926
And what we want to is know, well, how does wisdom grow?

141
00:14:39,966 --> 00:14:41,347
Well,

142
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:49,568
It's the very suffering that's being caused that makes us seek the end of suffering, which guides us towards wisdom.

143
00:14:50,330 --> 00:14:51,390
And that's how it works.

144
00:14:51,990 --> 00:14:58,357
So this is a really, shall we say, amazing insight that the Buddha had into our psychology.

145
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:05,225
So there we have it, these four clingings.

146
00:15:05,524 --> 00:15:07,167
Clinging to sensual pleasure,

147
00:15:07,822 --> 00:15:12,567
Clinging to rites and rituals as if they were enough to make us liberated.

148
00:15:13,149 --> 00:15:18,556
Clinging to views and opinions, the conceit of I am, I am right.

149
00:15:19,136 --> 00:15:21,620
And finally, this clinging to the sense of self.

150
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:29,091
And that one, of course, is the most difficult and the final clinging to be unclung.

151
00:15:30,131 --> 00:15:33,035
I'm getting very good with the word cling.

152
00:15:34,230 --> 00:15:46,164
So I can only hope my words have been of some assistance and that by your unclinging, you will be liberated from all suffering sooner rather than later.

